    24	
    25	# Kaggle packaging reuses the starter submission path
    26	
    27	## Claim
    28	
    29	The Task 7.4 brief (`ops/rebuild-2026-09-29/briefs/7.4-packaging.md`) plans
    30	Kaggriculture packaging on the starter's existing path. It adds no new build
    31	system:
    32	
    33	- `Dockerfile.kaggle` compiles the `abi3-py311` PyO3 extension inside Kaggle's
    34	  own amd64 image;
    35	- `scripts/build_kaggle_submission.sh` tars `owl`, `main.py` and the slim
    36	  checkpoint.
    37	
    38	The existing-concept search covered packaging, submission, Kaggle and runtime;
    39	no prior note covered this. The only earlier mention is the Evaluation
    40	Decision's rule that the owner decides submission, and this note follows it.
    41	
    42	## Reusable findings
    43	
    44	These come from sources read on 2026-09-29.
    45	
    46	**Timing.**
    47	
    48	- Kaggriculture gives 1 s per turn (`actTimeout`) plus a 60 s per-seat overage
    49	  bank. It inherits `runTimeout` 1200 s.
    50	- Kaggle staff say production runs the two agents in parallel. The local
    51	  file-agent runner is sequential.
    52	- A file agent is `exec`'d lazily inside the first timed call. Imports, weight
    53	  load and warm-up all bill to turn 0. `sys.path` includes the agent directory
    54	  only during that `exec`.
    55	
    56	**Loader behaviour.**
    57	
    58	- The loader picks the **last callable** in `main.py`.
    59	- It injects `configuration["__raw_path__"]`. Passing the Kaggle configuration
    60	  straight to the Rust envelope would be rejected (`config.rs`: only
    61	  `actTimeout`, `runTimeout` and `seed` are allowed as extra keys). The agent
    62	  must build the game configuration from an allowlist.
    63	
    64	**Encoding.**
    65	
    66	- The only merged Kaggriculture binding needs both seats' private state. A
    67	  live agent has one seat's view.
    68	- The brief adds a seat binding that calls the same `write_seat` as training.
    69	  It rejects a pure-Python encoder unless the extension is proven unloadable
    70	  on Kaggle.
    71	
    72	**Failure modes.**
    73	
    74	- An exception or a timeout loses the episode, and a dict that fails the
    75	  JSON schema becomes `INVALID`. A non-dict return (`None`, a list, a string)
    76	  does **not** fail: Kaggle's `default_schema` silently replaces it with the
    77	  default PASS action (`utils.py:150–195`). The brief therefore validates
    78	  every raw returned action, by structure and a native encode round trip,
    79	  before Kaggle normalizes it.
    80	- At termination the interpreter overwrites every seat's status and reward
    81	  (`kaggriculture.py:960–963`), so a fault on the final call ends as `DONE`.
    82	  Final statuses prove nothing; the harness captures each call's result
    83	  itself.
    84	- The brief keeps Isaiah's catch-all default action at the process boundary.
    85	  A strict mode re-raises for every test, benchmark and qualifying episode.
    86	
    87	**Competition rules.**
    88	
    89	- Only the latest 2 submissions count for the final leaderboard.
    90	- The Kaggle MCP page read returned the FAQ's resource values as unrendered
    91	  placeholders. The indexed official FAQ, read independently in both Codex
    92	  reviews, lists 1.6 vCPUs, 6.5 GiB RAM, 8 GiB disk and a 100 MiB submission
    93	  size; the brief designs to these and plans a dated receipt.
    94	
    95	## Consequence
    96	
    97	- Packaging work must reuse the starter's scripts, parameterized by
    98	  entrypoint.
    99	- Latency must be measured on amd64 inside the Kaggle image, not inferred
   100	  from the Mac or from GPU throughput.
   101	- Any submission stays the owner's decision, made with the displacement rule
   102	  in view.
   103	
   104	## Limits
   105	
   106	Nothing here is verified by execution. The following were **not** done:
   107	
   108	- no build, binding, test, episode or timing ran;
   109	- no Docker image was pulled;
   110	- the production image (Kaggle's master Dockerfile says `python:v163`, while
   111	  the starter pins a May 2026 `python-simulations` digest) and its torch
   112	  version are unconfirmed;
   113	- the arithmetic latency estimate in the brief (about 10 GFLOP per padded
   114	  709-token row) is not a measurement.
   115	
   116	The brief lists these under "Open questions". Reopen this note when Task 7.4
   117	lands with receipts.
